*********** Delegates ***********
Delegate is a function pointer. It's a type that store memory address of a function.
In other words it's a pipeline between event and event handler / action.

Ex of inbuilt delegeta:-
<input type="text" onfocus="focus()"/>
function focus() {}

Here onfocus is the event and focus() method is event handler/ action.

Types:-
1. Single cast delegate
2. Multi cast delegate

1. Single cast delegate
	a delegate pointing to only one function();
	Ex-
	public delegate void NotificationHandler (string message);
	public void main (){
		NotificationHandler handler = new NotificationHandler(TextNotification);
		handler.Invoke(message);
	}
	
	public void TextNotification(string Message){}
	
2. Multi cast delegate	
	a multi cast delegate can refer to more than one delegate function using an invocation list.

Ex:-
publicstaticvoid Main(string[] args)   
{  
    EmailSentNotificationHandler del1 = newEmailSentNotificationHandler(WriteEmailSentNotificationOnConsole);  
    del1 += WriteEmailSentNotificationinLogFile;  
    del1 += WriteEmailSentNotificationinDatabase;  
    del1.Invoke();  
}

We can unsubscribe to a delegate function at any given point of time.
Ex- 
EmailSentNotification -= WriteEmailSentNotificationinDatabase; 

Anonymous function, lambda expression, Action<T> are advanced feature of delegate.
